<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    two Rust protection domains exchange typed messages.

    input_producer (standing in for an input PD) sends a fixed, synthetic
    sequence of canonical input events - nowayos-input InputEvent records,
    encoded by nowayos-ipc - through a ring in shared memory, and notifies
    input_consumer (standing in for a UI PD). The consumer copies each record
    into private memory, then validates, decodes and reports it. One record is
    deliberately malformed and must be rejected. The ring holds four records,
    so the eleven-record sequence laps it: the consumer notifies the producer
    each time it has drained the ring.

    Narrowest rights: the ring spans two regions so that each PD can write
    only what it owns.

        input_ring_messages   producer index + slots   producer rw, consumer r
        input_ring_control    consumer index           consumer rw, producer r

    Each map binds a nowayos-region symbol pair whose name states the rights
    the map must grant: nowayos_rw_region_N_* only on perms="rw",
    nowayos_ro_region_N_* only on perms="r" (pds/region/src/lib.rs). No PD
    maps anything else; neither has a device, an interrupt or a protected
    procedure. A change here is a security change.

    Scheduling, both ways round: the producer runs above the consumer, as an
    input PD would run above the UI, so a busy UI cannot starve input; and
    the producer's budget is half its period (5 ms of every 10 ms), so an
    input PD that is compromised or stuck in a loop cannot starve the UI
    either. Placeholder values, to be tuned with the input PDs. The
    consumer keeps Microkit's default (budget = period = 1 ms: unthrottled),
    being the lower priority.

    Who prints when: on a real UART the producer's printing is real
    work - a line takes about 8 ms at 115200 baud - so its budget can hold
    it mid-line, and the consumer then runs. The kernel's debug output is
    not whole lines across PDs, so the consumer prints only while it holds
    the turn: the producer prints its lines, then notifies, and the
    consumer's start-up line waits for that first notification; the
    consumer prints its lines, then notifies back. The two never print at
    once, so the order the expect file checks holds on the Pi 5 too.

    Every grant above is also listed in ipc-demo.grants, which
    tools/check-system compares with this file and with the kernel objects
    the Microkit tool generates (make image, make qemu-test).
-->
<system>
    <memory_region name="input_ring_messages" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="input_ring_control" size="0x1000" />

    <protection_domain name="input_producer" priority="2" budget="5000" period="10000"
                       stack_size="0x10000">
        <program_image path="nowayos-ipc-producer.elf" />
        <map mr="input_ring_messages" vaddr="0x4000_0000" perms="rw"
             setvar_vaddr="nowayos_rw_region_0_vaddr" setvar_size="nowayos_rw_region_0_size" />
        <map mr="input_ring_control" vaddr="0x4000_1000" perms="r"
             setvar_vaddr="nowayos_ro_region_0_vaddr" setvar_size="nowayos_ro_region_0_size" />
    </protection_domain>

    <protection_domain name="input_consumer" priority="1" stack_size="0x10000">
        <program_image path="nowayos-ipc-consumer.elf" />
        <map mr="input_ring_messages" vaddr="0x4000_0000" perms="r"
             setvar_vaddr="nowayos_ro_region_0_vaddr" setvar_size="nowayos_ro_region_0_size" />
        <map mr="input_ring_control" vaddr="0x4000_1000" perms="rw"
             setvar_vaddr="nowayos_rw_region_0_vaddr" setvar_size="nowayos_rw_region_0_size" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- Notifications both ways: "records waiting" and "ring drained". -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="input_producer" id="0" />
        <end pd="input_consumer" id="0" />
    </channel>
</system>
